readings.c1s1:
{{smaller|''Sabbath'' or ''Great Saturday'' has been a name for Easter eve since very early times in both East and West.—'''Holy Week,''' in the ecclesiastical year, the week immediately preceding Easter Sunday: sometimes also called ''Passion Week.''—'''Miserere week.''' See ''miserere.''—'''New week.''' See ''new.''—'''Parson’s week.''' See ''parson.''—'''Passion Week.''' See ''passion.''—'''Procession week, Rogation week.''' See ''rogation.''—'''The feast of weeks,''' a Jewish festival lasting seven weeks—that is, a “week of weeks” after the Passover. It corresponds to Pentecost or Whitsuntide. See ''Pentecost'', 1.—'''This (that) day week.''' See ''day''<sup>1</sup>.}}

{{smaller block|''This day-week'' you will be alone.
{{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Shirley, xxvi.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Week about.''' See ''about.''—'''Week’s day,''' that day of last week or of next week which corresponds to the present day.}}

{{smaller block|I mene if God please to be at Salisburie the ''wekesdaie'' at night before Easterdaie; where for divers respectes I would gladlie speake w<sup>th</sup> you.
{{right|''Darrell Papers'' (1582) (H. Hall, Society in Elizabethan Age).}}}}

'''week<sup>2</sup>†''', ''n.'' An obsolete form of ''wick''<sup>1</sup>.

'''week<sup>3</sup>''' (wēk), ''n.'' [Sc. also ''weik'', ''wick''; a var. of ''wike''<sup>1</sup>.] A corner; an angle: as, the ''weeks'' of the mouth or the eye.

{{smaller block|The men of the world say we will sell the truth; we will let them ken that we will hing by the ''wicks'' of the mouth for the least point of truth.
{{right|''M. Bruce'', Soul-Confirmation, p. 18. (''Jamieson.'')}}}}

'''week-day''' (wēk′dā), ''n.'' [E. dial. ''weekyday''; < ME. *''wekeday'', < AS. ''wicdæg'', ''wucdæg'' = Icel. ''vikudagr''; as ''week''<sup>1</sup> + ''day''<sup>1</sup>.] Any day of the week except Sunday: often used adjectively.

{{smaller block|She loues Preaching better then Praying, and of Preachers Lecturers, and thinkes the ''Weeke-dayes'' Exercise farre more edifying then the Sundaies.
{{right|''Bp. Earle'', Micro-cosmographie, A Shee precise Hypocrite.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
''wael'', a whirlpool, = MLG. ''wēl'', a pool.] A whirlpool.

'''weel<sup>2</sup>''' (wēl), ''n.'' [Also ''weal''; cf. ''willy'', a willow basket, < ''willy'', a var. of ''willow'': see ''willow''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' A kind of trap or snare for fish. [Obsolete or provincial.]
{{smaller block|Fishing is a kind of hunting by water, be it with nets, ''weeles'', baits, angling. {{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 310.}}}}
{{smaller block|Diog. Laert. tells us that it was a saying of Socrates that young batchelers desirous of marriage were like to fishes who play about the ''weele'', and gladly would get in, when on the contrary they that are within strive how they should get out. {{right|''Heywood'', Anna and Phillis (Works, ed. [Pearson, 1874, VI. 310).}}}}
{{smaller block|In our river Ishnia eel-pouts were caught as well as crucians and crawfish; the last tumbled of themselves in the ''weels'' set for them, or into ordinary baskets. {{right|''Harper’s Mag.'', LXXVIII. 379.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''her.'', a bearing representing a kind of eel-pot or fish-pot, composed of strips or slats with open spaces between. Sometimes the number of these slats is mentioned in the blazon.

'''weel<sup>3</sup>''' (wēl), ''adv.'' and ''a.'' A Scotch form of ''well''<sup>2</sup>.

'''weem''' (wēm), ''n.'' [Cf. Gael. ''uamha'', a cave.] An earth-house; an artificial cave or subterranean building. [Scotch.]

'''ween†''' (wēn), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wene'', ''wen'', < AS. ''wēn'', f., ''wēna'', m., hope, weening, expectation, = OS. ''wān'' = OFries. ''wēn'', hope, = D. ''waan'', opinion, conjecture, = OHG. MHG. ''wān'', G. ''wahn'', illusion, false hope, = Icel. ''vān'', expectation, = Goth. ''wēns'', expectation; from the root of ''win'': see ''win''.] Doubt; conjecture.

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>For lyf and deth, withouten ''wene'',
Is in his hande. {{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 4596.}}</poem>}}

'''ween''' (wēn), ''v.'' [< ME. ''wenen'', < AS. ''wēnan'' (pret. ''wende'', pp. ''wende'', ''wente''), hope, expect, imagine, = OS. ''wānian'' = OFries. ''wēna'' = D. ''wanen'', think, fancy, = LG. ''wanen'', fancy, = OHG. ''wānan'', ''wānnan'', MHG. ''wænen'', G. ''wähnen'' = Icel. ''vāna'', hope (cf. Sw. ''vänta'' = Dan. ''vente''), = Goth. ''wēnjan'', expect; from the noun.] To be of opinion; have the notion; think; imagine; suppose. [Archaic.]
{{smaller block|And whan thei wil fighte, thei wille schokken hem to gidre in a plomp, that, zif there be 20000 men, men schalle not ''wenen'' that there be scant 10000.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 252.}}
<poem>But trewely I ''wende'', as in this cas,
Naught have agilt, ne doon to love trespas.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 462.}}</poem>
Prosperitie . . . may be discontinued by moe waies than you would afore haue ''went''.
{{right|''Sir T. More'', Cumfort against Tribulation (1573), fol. 34.}}
Earle Robert would needes set forward, ''weening'' to get all the glory to himselfe before the comming of the hoste.
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', II. 35.}}
<poem>Ye ''ween'' to hear a melting tale
Of two true lovers in a dale.
{{right|''Scott'', L. of L. M., ii. 29.}}

Though never a dream the roses sent
Of science or love’s compliment,
I ''ween'' they smelt as sweet.
{{right|''Mrs. Browning'', Deserted Garden.}}</poem>}}

'''weenong-tree''' (wē′nong-tre), ''n.'' See ''Tetrameles''.

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''weep'''<sup>1</sup> (wēp), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''wept'', ppr. ''weeping''. [< ME. ''wepen'', ''weopen'' (pret. ''weep'', ''wep'', ''weop'', ''wiep'', ''wip'', pl. ''wepen'', ''wepe'', ''wopen'', later ''wepte''), weep, wail, shed tears, < AS. ''wēpan'' (pret. ''weóp''), cry aloud, wail, = OS. ''wōpian'', cry aloud, = OFries. ''wēpa'' = OHG. ''wuofan'', ''wuofjan'' (pret. ''wiof''), MHG. ''wuofen'', ''wüefen'' = Icel. ''œpa'' (pret. ''œpta''), cry, shout, = Goth. ''wōpjan'' (pret. ''wōpida''), cry out, weep; from a noun, AS. ''wōp'', clamor, outcry, = OS. ''wōp'' = OHG. ''wuof'', ''wuaf'', outcry, lament, = Icel. ''ōp'', a shout; cf. Russ. ''vopite'', sob, wail, lament. Not connected with E. ''whoop'', which is prop. ''hoop''.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To express sorrow, grief, or anguish by outcry; wail; lament; in more modern usage, to shed tears.
{{smaller block|Thei of the Coutree seyn that Adam and Eve ''wepten'' upon that Mount an 100 Zeer, whan thei weren dryven out of Paradys. {{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 199.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>In al this world ther nis so cruwel herte . . .
That nolde have ''wopen'' for hire peynes smerte;
So tenderly she ''wepte'' both eve and morwe.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, v. 724.}}}}
{{smaller block|To whom he sayde, “''Wepe'' ye not vpon me, ye doughters of Jherusalem, but ''wepe'' ye vpon your self and vpon your children.” {{right|''Sir R. Guylforde'', Pylgrymage, p. 28.}}}}
{{smaller block|They all ''wept'' sore, and fell on Paul’s neck, and kissed him. {{right|Acts xx. 37.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Then they for sudden joy did ''weep''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 4. 191 (song).}}}}
{{smaller block|The Indian elephant is known sometimes to ''weep''. {{right|''Darwin'', Express. of Emotions, p. 167.}}}}

'''2.''' To drop or flow as tears.
{{smaller block|<poem>The blood ''weeps'' from my heart.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iv. 4. 58.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''3.''' To let fall drops; drop water; drip; hence, to rain.
{{smaller block|When heaven doth ''weep'', doth not the earth o’erflow?
{{right|''Shak.'', Tit. And., iii. 1. 222.}}}}

'''4.''' To give out moisture; be very damp.
{{smaller block|<poem>Clayes ''wepe''
Uncertainly, whoos teres beth right swete.</poem>
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 173.}}
It is a delicious place for prospect and y<sup>e</sup> thicketts, but the soile cold and ''weeping'' clay.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Feb. 17, 1662.}}}}

'''5.''' To have drooping branches; be pendent; droop: as, a ''weeping'' tree; the ''weeping'' willow. {{smaller|—'''To weep Irish''', to express or affect sympathetic grief by wailing and shedding tears; keen.}}
{{smaller block|Surely the Egyptians did not ''weep-Irish'' with faigned and mercenary tears.
{{right|''Fuller'', Pisgah Sight, II. xii. 15. (''Davies.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Weeping ash''', the variety ''pendula'' of the European ash, ''Fraxinus excelsior'', having the branches arching downward instead of upward.—'''Weeping birch''', a variety of the white birch, ''Betula alba'', of a weeping habit, common in Europe, and often cultivated for ornament. Its shoots when young are quite smooth, but when mature are of a bright chestnut-brown, covered with little white warts.—'''Weeping eczema''', eczema attended with considerable exudation; moist eczema.—'''Weeping grass''', a grass, ''Microlæna (Ehrharta) stipoides'', of Australia and New Zealand, so called doubtless from the form of its panicle. It is a perennial grass, keeping green through the year, and valued for grazing. ''Mueller'', Select Extra-trop. Plants.—'''Weeping oak.''' See ''oak''.—'''Weeping pipe''', a small pipe connected with a tank or water-closet supply-pipe, and designed to allow a little water to escape at intervals so as to preserve the seal in traps.—'''Weeping poplar.''' See ''poplar''.—'''Weeping rock''', a porous rock from which water oozes.—'''Weeping sinew''', a gathering of fluid in the synovial sheath of a tendon; ganglion.—'''Weeping willow.''' See ''willow''<sup>1</sup>.}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To lament; bewail; bemoan.
{{smaller block|<poem>Pensive she sat, revolving fates to come,
And ''wept'' her godlike son’s approaching doom.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, xxiv. 114.}}
<poem>Nor is it
Wiser to ''weep'' a true occasion lost,
But trim our sails, and let old bygones be.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, iv.}}
To ''weep'' his obsequies. {{right|''Dryden'', Æneid, ix. 648.}}}}
'''2.''' To shed or let fall drop by drop, as tears; give out in drops.
{{smaller block|Sithen thou hast ''wepen'' [var. ''wopen''] many a drope.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, i. 941.}}
Sir Gawein that ther-of hadde grete pite hit toke with gladde chere and myri, and ''wepte'' right tendirly water with his iyen vndir his helme.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 477.}}
<poem>''Weep'' your tears
Into the channel.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., i. 1. 63.}}
Groves whose rich trees ''wept'' odorous gums and balm.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 248.}}}}
'''3.''' To spend or consume in weeping; exhaust in tears: usually followed by ''away'', ''out'', or the like.
{{smaller block|''Weep'' my life ''away''. {{right|''Tennyson'', Merlin and Vivien.}}
<poem>I could ''weep''
My spirit from mine eyes.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., iv. 3. 99.}}}}
'''To weep millstones†.''' See ''millstone''.

'''weep<sup>1</sup>''' (wēp), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wepe'', ''wep'', a later form, after the verb, of ''wop'', < AS. ''wōp'', clamor, cry: see ''weep''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1†.''' Weeping; a fit of weeping.
{{smaller block|She began to breste a ''wepe'' anon.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, ii. 408.}}
Wid reweli lote, and sorwe, and ''wep''.
{{right|''Genesis and Exodus'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2328.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
'''2.''' Exudation; sweat, as of a gum-tree; a leak, as in the joint of a pipe. [Obsolete, colloq., or trade use.]

'''weep<sup>2</sup>†''', ''n.'' [Imitative.] Same as ''peweep'' for ''pewit.'' Also ''wype, wipe.''

'''weepable†''' (wē′pa-bl), ''a.'' [Early mod. E. ''wepeable''; < ''weep''<sup>1</sup> + ''-able.''] Exciting or moving to tears; lamentable; grievous. ''Bp. Pecock.''

'''weeper''' (wē′pėr), ''n.'' [< ''weep''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' One who weeps; one who sheds tears; specifically, a hired mourner at a funeral.
{{smaller block|If you have served God in a holy life, send away the women and the ''weepers''; tell them it is as much intemperance to weep too much as to laugh too much.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Holy Living, ii. 6.}}
<poem>Laughing is easy, but the wonder lies
What store of brine supplied the ''weeper’s'' eyes.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Juvenal’s Satires, x. 46.}}}}
'''2.''' Something worn conventionally as a badge of mourning. {{smaller|(''a'') A strip of white linen or muslin worn on the end of the sleeve like a cuff. The term is also used for the band of crape worn as a mark of mourning.}}
{{smaller block|Our . . . mourners clap bits of muslin on their sleeves, and these are called ''weepers.''
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Citizen of the World, xcvi.}}
There was not a widow in all the country who went to such an expense for black bombazine. She had her beautiful hair confined in crimped caps, and her ''weepers'' came over her elbows. {{right|''Thackeray'', Bluebeard’s Ghost.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') A long hatband, like a scarf, of crape or other black stuff, worn by men at a funeral.}}
{{smaller block|It is a funereal street, Old Parr Street, certainly; the carriages which drive there ought to have feathers on the roof, and the butlers who open the doors should wear ''weepers.'' {{right|''Thackeray'', Philip, ii.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller|(''c'') The long black crape veil worn by a widow in her weeds.}}
{{smaller block|Most thankful I shall be to see you with a couple o’ pounds’ worth less of crape. . . . If anybody was to marry me flattering himself I should wear these hijeous ''weepers'' two years for him, he’d be deceived by his own vanity, that ’s all. {{right|''George Eliot'', Middlemarch, lxxx.}}}}
'''3.''' Anything resembling a weeper in senses 1 and 2 in shape or use.
{{smaller block|The firs were hung with ''weepers'' of black-green moss. {{right|''B. Taylor'', Northern Travel, p. 169.}}}}
{{smaller block|The eyes with which it [the aqueduct tunnel] weeps are rightly called ''weepers'', being small rectangular openings in the side walls, through which all the water collected and collecting on the outside of the masonry pours into the inside. {{right|''New York Tribune'', February 2, 1890.}}}}
'''4.''' The South American capuchin monkey, ''Cebus capucinus.''

'''weepful†''' (wēp′fu̇l), ''a.'' [< ''weep''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'', + ''-ful.''] Full of weeping; mournful. ''Wyclif.''

'''weeping''' (wē′ping), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wepinge'', ''wepynge''; verbal n. of ''weep''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] Wailing; lamentation; shedding of tears.
{{smaller block|With myche ''wepyng'' & woo thes wordes ho said. {{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 8489.}}}}
{{smaller block|There shall be ''weeping'' and gnashing of teeth. {{right|Mat. viii. 12.}}}}

'''weeping-cross''' (wē′ping-krôs), ''n.'' A cross, often of stone, erected on or by the side of a highway, at which penitential devotions were performed.
